<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>The 7-Day Money Reset</title>
<style>
{{ base_css }}
/* ---- lead magnet: printable, light pages ---- */
.page{ padding:0.6in 0.75in 0.8in; }
.folio{ left:0.75in; right:0.75in; }
.band{ margin:-0.6in -0.75in 0; padding:0.55in 0.75in 0.45in; background:var(--ink); color:var(--cream); }
.band .tag{ color:var(--gold); letter-spacing:.2em; text-transform:uppercase; font-weight:700; font-size:8.5pt; }
.band h1{ font-size:44pt; font-weight:900; letter-spacing:-.035em; line-height:.98; margin:14px 0 10px; }
.band h1 em{ font-style:normal; color:var(--green); }
.band .sub{ font-size:12.5pt; color:#cfd8cf; max-width:5.9in; line-height:1.4; margin:0; }
.week{ display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(7,minmax(0,1fr)); gap:6px; margin:14px 0 8px; }
.week > div{ border:1.5px solid var(--ink); border-radius:9px; padding:8px 7px 9px; background:#fff; min-height:1.05in; position:relative; }
.week .d{ font-size:7.5pt; text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.12em; font-weight:800; color:var(--green-d); }
.week .t{ font-weight:700; font-size:9pt; line-height:1.25; margin-top:4px; }
.week .m{ position:absolute; bottom:7px; left:7px; font-size:7.5pt; color:var(--muted); }
.week .bx{ position:absolute; bottom:7px; right:7px; width:13px; height:13px; border:1.5px solid var(--ink); border-radius:3px; }
.day{ margin:0 0 6px; }
.dayhead{ display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:12px; border-bottom:2px solid var(--ink); padding-bottom:5px; margin-bottom:8px; }
.dayhead .no{ font-family:"Inter Display","Inter",sans-serif; font-weight:900; font-size:30pt; line-height:.9; color:var(--green); letter-spacing:-.03em; }
.dayhead h2{ font-size:19pt; flex:1; margin:0; }
.dayhead .time{ font-size:8.5pt; font-weight:700; color:var(--muted); white-space:nowrap; }
.why{ color:var(--muted); font-size:10pt; margin-bottom:6px; }
.check{ margin:4px 0 8px; }
.check li{ margin-bottom:4px; }
.fill{ border:1.5px dashed #b9b19c; border-radius:10px; padding:8px 12px 10px; background:#fff; }
.fill .kicker{ margin-bottom:4px; }
.fill table{ margin:0; font-size:9pt; }
.fill th{ background:var(--cream); padding:4px 8px; border-bottom:1.5px solid var(--ink); }
.fill td{ height:22px; border-bottom:1px solid var(--line); }
.line{ display:flex; gap:8px; align-items:flex-end; margin:7px 0; font-size:9.5pt; }
.line span.u{ flex:1; border-bottom:1px solid #9c9583; height:16px; }
.us, .line span.us{ width:1.1in; border-bottom:1px solid #9c9583; height:16px; display:inline-block; }
.opt{ white-space:nowrap; display:inline-flex; align-items:center; gap:6px; margin-right:18px; font-weight:600; }
.opt i{ width:12px; height:12px; border:1.5px solid var(--ink); border-radius:3px; display:inline-block; }
.sep{ height:16px; }
.next{ border:2px solid var(--green-d); border-radius:12px; padding:14px 18px; background:var(--tint); }
.next h3{ margin-top:0; }
.small{ font-size:8.5pt; color:var(--muted); }
.formula{ font-size:9.5pt; }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<!-- ============ 1. START ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="band">
    <div class="tag">Money Made Simple &middot; Free printable</div>
    <h1>The 7-Day<br><em>Money Reset</em></h1>
    <p class="sub">One small action a day, 10 to 25 minutes each. In a week you'll know exactly where your money is, have a safety buffer started and a plan for your debt.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:18px">How it works</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Print this, or fill it in on screen.</strong> Each day has one action, a short checklist and a space to write things down.</li>
    <li><strong>Do the days in order.</strong> Each one builds on the last. Missed a day? Just pick up where you left off.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep it rough.</strong> Round numbers are fine. The goal is a clear picture, not a perfect spreadsheet.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Your week at a glance</h3>
  <div class="week">
    {% for d in days %}<div><div class="d">Day {{ loop.index }}</div><div class="t">{{ d.short }}</div><div class="m">~{{ d.mins }} min</div><span class="bx"></span></div>{% endfor %}
  </div>
  <p class="small">Tick the box when a day is done.</p>

  <h3>What you'll need</h3>
  <div class="grid2">
    <ul class="check" style="margin:0">
      <li>Your phone or laptop, logged in to your bank and card apps</li>
      <li>Your last two months of statements (the apps have them)</li>
    </ul>
    <ul class="check" style="margin:0">
      <li>A pen, if you printed this</li>
      <li>Your calendar app, for Days 3 and 7</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="box tip" style="margin-top:16px">
    <strong>Why seven small days instead of one big afternoon?</strong> Because a 15-minute task is easy to start, and starting is the hard part. By Day 7 you'll have done every piece of a working money system without ever sitting down for a dreaded "budget day".
  </div>

  <div class="box warn">
    <p class="small" style="color:var(--ink);margin:0">General education, not financial advice. It doesn't know your situation. If you're behind on payments or dealing with collections, a non-profit credit counselor (in the US, an NFCC member agency) is a good, low-cost next step.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="folio"><span>The 7-Day Money Reset &middot; Money Made Simple</span><span>1</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2. DAYS 1-2 ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="day">
    <div class="dayhead"><span class="no">1</span><h2>Take a money snapshot</h2><span class="time">~20 min</span></div>
    <p class="why">You can't plan from a vague feeling. Today you write down where every dollar is right now. No judging, no fixing. It's just your starting line.</p>
    <ul class="check">
      <li>Log in to every account: checking, savings, credit cards, loans, retirement.</li>
      <li>Write each balance below. For debts, add the interest rate (APR). It's on the statement.</li>
      <li>Find your <strong>monthly take-home pay</strong>: what actually lands in your account after tax, not your salary.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="fill">
      <div class="kicker">My snapshot &middot; date: ________</div>
      <table>
        <tr><th style="width:38%">Account</th><th style="width:22%">Type</th><th class="r" style="width:20%">Balance</th><th class="r">APR (debts)</th></tr>
        {% for i in range(6) %}<tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>{% endfor %}
      </table>
      <div class="line"><strong>Monthly take-home pay:</strong> $<span class="us"></span></div>
      <div class="line"><strong style="margin-right:6px">I'm paid:</strong> <span class="opt"><i></i>weekly</span><span class="opt"><i></i>every 2 weeks</span><span class="opt"><i></i>twice a month</span><span class="opt"><i></i>monthly</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="sep"></div>
  <div class="day">
    <div class="dayhead"><span class="no">2</span><h2>Cancel one subscription</h2><span class="time">~15 min</span></div>
    <p class="why">Recurring charges are the easiest money to get back, because cancelling once saves you every month after. Most people find at least one they forgot about.</p>
    <ul class="check">
      <li>Search your last two statements for: <em>subscription, monthly, renewal, membership, auto-pay</em>.</li>
      <li>Check your phone's app store subscriptions page (both iPhone and Android have one in settings).</li>
      <li>List them below, mark keep or cancel, and <strong>cancel at least one today</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="fill">
      <div class="kicker">My subscriptions</div>
      <table>
        <tr><th style="width:52%">Subscription</th><th class="r" style="width:18%">$ / month</th><th style="width:15%">Keep</th><th>Cancel</th></tr>
        {% for i in range(6) %}<tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>{% endfor %}
      </table>
      <div class="line"><strong>Saved per month:</strong> $<span class="us"></span> &nbsp;&times; 12 = $<span class="us"></span> a year</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="folio"><span>The 7-Day Money Reset &middot; Money Made Simple</span><span>2</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3. DAYS 3-4 ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="day">
    <div class="dayhead"><span class="no">3</span><h2>Build your bill calendar</h2><span class="time">~20 min</span></div>
    <p class="why">Late fees and missed payments usually come from forgetting, not from being broke. Payment history is the biggest single factor in common credit scores, so make "on time" automatic.</p>
    <ul class="check">
      <li>List every fixed bill and its due date: rent, utilities, phone, insurance, every debt's minimum.</li>
      <li>Turn on autopay for at least the <strong>minimum</strong> on every debt. You can always pay more by hand.</li>
      <li>Put any bill without autopay in your calendar, two days before it's due.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="fill">
      <div class="kicker">My bills</div>
      <table>
        <tr><th style="width:40%">Bill</th><th class="r" style="width:18%">Amount</th><th style="width:20%">Due (day of month)</th><th>Autopay on?</th></tr>
        {% for i in range(8) %}<tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>{% endfor %}
      </table>
      <div class="line"><strong>Total fixed bills per month:</strong> $<span class="us"></span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="sep"></div>
  <div class="day">
    <div class="dayhead"><span class="no">4</span><h2>Set three bank alerts</h2><span class="time">~10 min</span></div>
    <p class="why">Alerts catch problems while they're still small: a low balance before it overdraws, a strange charge before it becomes three. Every major bank app has them, usually under Settings &rarr; Alerts or Notifications.</p>
    <ul class="check">
      <li><strong>Low balance</strong> on checking, below $<span class="us" style="width:.6in"></span></li>
      <li><strong>Large transaction</strong> on every card and account, over $<span class="us" style="width:.6in"></span></li>
      <li><strong>Payment due</strong> reminders on each credit card (in the card's app), <span class="us" style="width:.4in"></span> days before.</li>
      <li>While you're there: check whether overdraft "coverage" is on, and decide if you want it.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="fill">
      <div class="kicker">My alerts</div>
      <table>
        <tr><th style="width:34%">Account or card</th><th style="width:30%">Alert</th><th class="r" style="width:18%">Threshold</th><th>Done</th></tr>
        {% for i in range(5) %}<tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>{% endfor %}
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="folio"><span>The 7-Day Money Reset &middot; Money Made Simple</span><span>3</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4. DAYS 5-6 ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="day">
    <div class="dayhead"><span class="no">5</span><h2>Start your emergency fund</h2><span class="time">~15 min</span></div>
    <p class="why">A small cash buffer is what stops a flat tire or a vet bill from turning into new credit card debt. The amount matters less than making it automatic.</p>
    <ul class="check">
      <li>Open a separate savings account, or a savings "bucket" inside your bank, and name it <em>Emergency</em>.</li>
      <li>Check it's insured: FDIC for banks, NCUA for credit unions (you can look a bank up at fdic.gov).</li>
      <li>Set an <strong>automatic transfer on payday</strong>. Even $10 or $25 is a real start.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="fill">
      <div class="line"><strong>Account:</strong> <span class="u"></span></div>
      <div class="line"><strong>Auto-transfer:</strong> $<span class="us"></span> every <span class="u"></span></div>
      <div class="line"><strong>First goal:</strong> $<span class="us"></span> by <span class="u"></span> <span style="font-size:8.5pt;color:var(--muted)">(try $500 first, then one month of bills)</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="sep"></div>
  <div class="day">
    <div class="dayhead"><span class="no">6</span><h2>List your debts, pick an order</h2><span class="time">~20 min</span></div>
    <p class="why">Paying a bit extra on everything feels responsible but spreads your effort thin. Pay the minimum on all of them, then send every extra dollar to <strong>one</strong> target. When it's gone, roll its payment into the next.</p>
    <div class="grid2" style="gap:12px;margin-bottom:8px">
      <div class="card"><div class="lbl">Avalanche</div><strong>Highest interest rate first.</strong> <span style="font-size:9pt">Costs the least interest overall.</span></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="lbl">Snowball</div><strong>Smallest balance first.</strong> <span style="font-size:9pt">Quick wins that keep you going.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="fill">
      <table>
        <tr><th style="width:36%">Debt</th><th class="r" style="width:20%">Balance</th><th class="r" style="width:14%">APR</th><th class="r" style="width:16%">Minimum</th><th class="r">Order</th></tr>
        {% for i in range(6) %}<tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>{% endfor %}
      </table>
      <div class="line"><strong>My method:</strong> <span class="opt"><i></i>Avalanche</span><span class="opt"><i></i>Snowball</span> <strong>Target debt:</strong> <span class="u"></span></div>
      <div class="line"><strong>Extra I'll pay on it each month:</strong> $<span class="us"></span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="box tip"><strong>Can't decide?</strong> If one debt has a much higher rate than the rest (say 25% vs 7%), avalanche usually saves real money. If the rates are close, pick snowball and enjoy the early wins. Either way, the plan you stick with beats the perfect one you don't.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="folio"><span>The 7-Day Money Reset &middot; Money Made Simple</span><span>4</span></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5. DAY 7 + NEXT ============ -->
<section class="page">
  <div class="day">
    <div class="dayhead"><span class="no">7</span><h2>Find your weekly spending number</h2><span class="time">~25 min</span></div>
    <p class="why">Everything you've done this week feeds one number: how much you can spend each week on groceries, eating out, gas and fun, with bills, savings and debt already handled.</p>
    <div class="fill">
      <div class="line"><span style="width:2.6in">Monthly take-home pay <span class="small">(Day 1)</span></span>$<span class="us"></span></div>
      <div class="line"><span style="width:2.6in">&minus; Fixed bills <span class="small">(Day 3)</span></span>$<span class="us"></span></div>
      <div class="line"><span style="width:2.6in">&minus; Emergency fund transfer <span class="small">(Day 5)</span></span>$<span class="us"></span></div>
      <div class="line"><span style="width:2.6in">&minus; Extra debt payment <span class="small">(Day 6)</span></span>$<span class="us"></span></div>
      <div class="line"><span style="width:2.6in">&minus; Irregular costs &divide; 12 <span class="small">(gifts, car, renewals)</span></span>$<span class="us"></span></div>
      <div class="line"><span style="width:2.6in"><strong>= Monthly spending pool</strong></span>$<span class="us"></span></div>
      <div class="line"><span style="width:2.6in"><strong>&times; 12 &divide; 52 = my weekly number</strong></span>$<span class="us" style="border-bottom:2px solid var(--green-d)"></span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:6px">Why &times;12&divide;52 and not &divide;4? A month is about 4.33 weeks. Dividing by 4 hands you a week of spending money you don't have. If the pool comes out negative, trim the extra debt payment or savings first, then look at the biggest bills.</p>
    <ul class="check">
      <li>Put a <strong>recurring 20-minute "money check-in"</strong> in your calendar, same time every week.</li>
      <li>At each check-in: how much of this week's number is left? Anything odd on the statements? Any bills coming?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="box dark" style="margin-top:12px">
    <div class="kicker">You did it</div>
    <p style="margin:0;font-size:11pt">Seven days ago this was a vague worry. Now you know your numbers, your bills run on time, your buffer is growing on autopilot and your debt has a plan. That's a money system. The weekly check-in is how you keep it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="next">
    <h3>Want the spreadsheet version?</h3>
    <p style="margin-bottom:6px">If you'd like Day 7's math done for you every month, I made <strong>The 20-Minute Budget</strong>: a 14-page guide plus a spreadsheet that turns this reset into a 20-minute weekly routine, with a weekly check-in tab and a debt payoff planner. It's my own product, ${{ budget_price }}, and the link is on the Money Made Simple link page.</p>
    <p class="small" style="margin:0">Totally optional. Everything in this reset works on paper.</p>
  </div>

  <p class="small" style="margin-top:12px">You're getting this because you joined the Money Made Simple weekly email: one money move a week, no spam, unsubscribe any time with the link at the bottom of every email.</p>
  <p class="small">General education, not financial, tax or investment advice. Bank features and names of settings vary. &copy; Money Made Simple. Free to print for your own use.</p>
  <div class="folio"><span>The 7-Day Money Reset &middot; Money Made Simple</span><span>5</span></div>
</section>

</body>
</html>
